00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:01.270 align:start position:0%
 
it's andy burnham thanks ever so much  for uh taking the time this morning  i think i'm right in saying you  acknowledge the necessity  of these measures but you think the  manner in which they were announced was  a little bit clumsy  that's a pretty good summary um but yes  on the substance we  we do uh accept that uh these steps are  needed  they're modest steps um you know we're  asking people  uh not to have visitors at home um  if they go to the pub to stick within  their own household  steps that hopefully will prevent  much more severe restrictions if we we  take firm action at this time and the  reason for it colin is  the picture changed in greater  manchester over the last week we're  watching the data very very closely like  the government is  so when the government contacted me to  say look we think something is happening  here  i could already see that it was and it  wasn't just in one locality  there was a change across all of our  boroughs and that's why we said to the  government that they were  they were right to take quick action  yeah we'll come into the measures  uh specifically in a moment but just in  terms of how you make these  announcements  it's quite difficult for the government  isn't it really because i know they were  criticized for instance with the  quarantine period in the fact they  announced that on a saturday evening  people had five hours notice but there  was a reason for that as well to stop  people sort of circumventing the rules  flying back early  there isn't there isn't a perfect way of  making these announcements or is there  no i don't think there is a perfect way  and i understand how hard this is  for government ministers having been one  uh myself  and i think at the moment they're  adopting a  feels like a new strategy of the minute  they see something they are taking  decisive action and i think that's  probably the right way to go but here's  the one  caveat i would place on it they they  have a habit of saying something and  then it being a few hours until the  detail emerges and that certainly was  the case  last night and you know later on last  night a lot of people i think felt very  uncertain  as to what exactly was being announced  so  what i would say to them is i understand  the need to make announcements i  understand the need for decisive action  but when ministers go in front of the  cameras make sure you've got the detail  ready to go exactly at the same time  yeah  i i'm sure they might say it's it's  difficult when you've got lots of  stakeholders involved and  if somebody knows something then they  might put it tweet it and suddenly the  message is out and it can look quite  ham-fisted can't it just in terms of the  enforceability of the thing  um to some degree this is going to be  devolved to local authorities  obviously uh local police forces as well  how enforceable do you think this is  this is going to prove clearly it's  going to be challenging  we're going to have to look to to people  themselves first to do the right thing  and to respect these new requirements  because they're being brought in for a  good reason that is to protect everybody  uh here let's get to the substance of it  as well though i think the reason the  government have chosen these particular  measures  is because of the evidence of a rising  cases in  households where there were gatherings  and i think that's why they wanted a  very clear statement on that particular  issue and this doesn't just relate to um  multi-generational households look where  people are living together  we've seen uh situations in parts of  greater monster where there's been a  rising cases amongst uh younger people  and that it's felt possibly is also  linked to  uh to house gatherings so i can  understand why the government is sending  a really clear message uh  on that particular issue uh and i would  say to everybody  the quicker we follow this advice  the quicker these restrictions will be  lifted i've agreed with the secretary of  state that they will be reviewed weekly  uh so you know this is a a preventative  step if you like  on the precautionary principle and i  think if we all  follow it it's not a massive change to  make and it's not a huge amount to ask  then i think we could um we could  quickly see these cases come back down  in the other direction  yeah as you say it's different  communities that are affected there's  a particular focus hasn't there on as  you say uh  homes multiple occupancy  intergenerational uh  habitation going on there um we've got  eid coming up this weekend  what is your message to people of south  asian ancestry  in in the two amongst the 2.8 million  mancunians you represent  uh as they approach this weekend it's  it's a difficult balancing act between  wanting to observe religious ceremonies  and rituals and  and pay homage to all that whilst  observing these new social distancing  rules  well certainly you know i recognize how  um disruptive this might be to people's  uh plans as you say with with the  uh this weekend uh people can still uh  go uh  to the mosque so there's no uh  restriction on on that  of course people would have to observe  distancing uh in the mosque but  there's been a lot of work going on to  ensure that the proper measures are in  place there  so it's not about preventing people  going to worship it is of course about  gatherings back at home uh and that  is something that nobody has kind of  wanted to have to do but  uh the circumstances have pretty much  demanded it  and it's true of everybody really it's  decent weather today here in greater  manchester we're  not always in a position to say that but  we are today and you know  we've got to say to everybody in all  parts of the the city region  you know plea well you can't don't  gather in your home um  because this is clearly a source of  spread of the virus  amongst all age groups actually and in  all geographies  and that's why the government  reluctantly i  accepted that they were they were right  to propose this measure  and i suppose that the blunt truth is if  this doesn't work you can expect  more draconian lockdown measures well we  don't want to see that and i've been  arguing  uh against that in many ways you know  that we need all of the data to stop  that happening so you will have heard me  on  your your programs over recent weeks  saying give us the data  let us see the picture and finally that  data came through  once the government kind of agreed to  what i was saying so i i could see  exactly what they could see  earlier this week and so could all of  the council leaders in greater  manchester  and we could see that there was a  problem that required firmer action  so when the health secretary called me i  wasn't surprised you know i kind of  was in the same mindset myself you know  what are we going to do here to quickly  nip this situation  in the bud so you know having called for  the data having had the ability to see  the picture  uh obviously i was in a position to  agree with what with what he was  with what he was saying uh andy burnham  very good to talk to you thank you  thanks very much

